CAT = "ieqfaProtection"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
("A level term assurance policy pays out:",
 "Only on death within the term", ["On death at any age at all", "On surrender after five years in force", "On retirement at age 66"],
 "Term assurance is pure protection: it pays the sum assured if the life assured dies within the term and has no cash value if they survive it."),
("Mortgage protection on a capital and interest (repayment) mortgage is usually arranged as:",
 "Decreasing term assurance", ["Level whole of life cover", "Unit-linked savings plan", "Income protection cover"],
 "Mortgage protection is decreasing term cover, with the sum assured falling roughly in line with the outstanding loan, so the mortgage is cleared on death."),
("Aoife's term policy has a conversion option. A year before it ends she is diagnosed with diabetes. The option lets her:",
 "Take new cover without a medical", ["Claim the sum assured at once", "Cancel and get all premiums back", "Move the cover to her husband"],
 "A conversion option lets the policyholder take out new whole of life or longer term cover on the same life without further medical evidence, which matters most when health has worsened."),
("Compared with term assurance for the same sum assured, a whole of life policy usually costs more because:",
 "A claim is certain to arise one day", ["It covers only accidental death", "It pays out only before age 60", "It never has a cash value at all"],
 "Whole of life cover lasts for life while premiums are paid, so the insurer will certainly pay a claim. Term cover pays only if death occurs within the term."),
("A Section 72 policy is designed to:",
 "Pay inheritance tax on an estate", ["Repay a car loan on job loss", "Fund a child's college fees", "Replace lost income during a long illness"],
 "A Section 72 policy, under the Capital Acquisitions Tax Consolidation Act 2003, is a whole of life policy whose proceeds are used to pay inheritance tax arising on the death."),
("A married couple want a policy that pays out only when the second of them dies. They need:",
 "Joint life, second death cover", ["Joint life, first death cover", "Two single life term policies", "Decreasing term cover"],
 "A joint life second death (last survivor) policy pays on the death of the survivor. It is often used to pay inheritance tax when the estate passes to children."),
("Niamh is diagnosed with a cancer that meets the definition in her specified illness policy. She receives:",
 "A lump sum, whether or not she works", ["An income only while off work", "Only the cost of her treatment", "A refund of premiums she paid"],
 "Specified (serious) illness cover pays a lump sum on diagnosis of an illness that meets the policy definition, regardless of whether the person can keep working."),
("Income protection (permanent health insurance) is designed to:",
 "Replace part of earnings in illness", ["Pay off the whole mortgage at once", "Give a lump sum on any diagnosis", "Cover the cost of private hospital stays"],
 "Income protection pays a regular income, a proportion of earnings, if the insured cannot work through illness or injury, after a deferred period and until recovery or the end of the term."),
("A longer deferred period on an income protection policy usually means:",
 "A lower premium", ["A higher premium", "A larger benefit", "No medical questions"],
 "The deferred period is the wait before benefit starts. The longer it is, the fewer claims the insurer pays, so the premium is lower."),
("Seán cannot work for eight months after an injury. His income protection policy includes waiver of premium. During that time:",
 "The insurer pays his premiums", ["His cover lapses until he returns", "His premiums are doubled later", "He must still pay every premium"],
 "Waiver of premium keeps the policy in force by having the insurer meet the premiums once the person has been unable to work for longer than the deferred period."),
("Indexation on a protection policy means:",
 "Cover and premium rise yearly", ["Cover falls each year to zero", "Premiums are fixed for life", "Benefits track a share index such as the ISEQ"],
 "Indexation increases the sum assured each year, usually with a matching premium increase, to help cover keep its real value against inflation."),
("A protection policy has reviewable premiums. This means the insurer:",
 "Can change the premium at reviews", ["Must keep the premium fixed for life", "Reviews the claim only after death", "Reviews the client's credit record each year"],
 "Reviewable premiums can rise at set review dates. Guaranteed premiums cannot change for the term, and usually cost more at the start."),
("An applicant with a history of heart disease is offered life cover at a higher premium. This is called:",
 "A premium loading", ["A waiver of premium", "An indexation option", "A conversion option"],
 "When underwriting finds a higher risk, the insurer may load the premium, apply an exclusion, postpone or decline, rather than offer standard rates."),
("A home should be insured for the cost of:",
 "Rebuilding it", ["Its market value", "Its mortgage balance", "Its original purchase price"],
 "Buildings cover should reflect the full reinstatement (rebuilding) cost, including site clearance and professional fees, not the market value, which includes the land."),
("Irish private health insurance is community rated. This means people on the same plan generally pay:",
 "The same premium whatever their health", ["Premiums based on their own health history", "Premiums based on their income", "Premiums set by their employer"],
 "Under the Health Insurance Acts, insurers must charge the same premium for a given plan regardless of age or health, apart from lifetime community rating loadings and permitted discounts."),
("Under open enrolment, an Irish health insurer must:",
 "Accept anyone who applies", ["Accept under-65s only", "Ask a medical questionnaire", "Accept people in one county"],
 "Open enrolment means health insurers must accept all applicants, whatever their age or health, though waiting periods may apply to pre-existing conditions."),
("Lifetime community rating loadings on health insurance apply to people who:",
 "First take out cover from age 35", ["Make a claim in their first year", "Switch insurer", "Pay their premium monthly"],
 "Lifetime community rating adds a loading to the premium of people who first buy health insurance aged 35 or over, to encourage taking cover earlier."),
("Payment protection insurance on a personal loan is designed to:",
 "Meet repayments in sickness or job loss", ["Pay off the whole loan if interest rates rise", "Cover damage to the item bought", "Repay the lender if the borrower moves"],
 "PPI pays loan repayments for a limited period if the borrower cannot work through illness, accident or, in some policies, involuntary unemployment."),
("A policyholder asks about surrendering a life policy early. Under the Code, the firm must tell him the surrender value and:",
 "If a secondary market exists", ["How much commission was paid", "The insurer's annual profit", "The value of his other policies with them"],
 "If a secondary market exists where such a policy could be sold, the firm must say so when giving details of the surrender value."),
("Under a policy with a no claims discount, the Code requires the insurer to give the consumer:",
 "The discount's terms and conditions", ["A copy of its reinsurance treaty", "The names of its other customers", "A guarantee that the discount will rise each year"],
 "Where a no claims discount applies, the insurer must give the consumer its terms and conditions, including how claims affect it."),
]
